cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below, and cite de Bruijn (1950) for the engine, Freire, Gonzalez-Riquelme, Munoz, Poblete and Salinas (arXiv:2610.27343) for the conditional statement, and OpenAI's quasi-Riemann hypothesis preprint for the 7/8 half-plane."
title: "Every real primitive Dirichlet L-function has de Bruijn-Newman constant at most 9/32: a kernel-checked proof in Lean 4"
authors:
  - family-names: Murphy
    given-names: Peter W.
license: Apache-2.0
version: v1.0.0
keywords:
  - de Bruijn-Newman constant
  - Dirichlet L-functions
  - real primitive characters
  - Gauss sums
  - heat flow
  - Lean 4
  - formal verification
abstract: >-
  For every real primitive Dirichlet character chi != 1, every zero of the de Bruijn-Newman heat flow of the
  completed L-function Lambda(s, chi) is real at every time t >= 9/32; classically, Lambda_chi <= 9/32. The
  statement is proved in Lean 4 (v4.34.1, Mathlib d13f23b) in one environment with OpenAI's kernel-checked
  theorem that Dirichlet L-functions do not vanish on Re s > 7/8 (openai/math at adc7f124), together with a
  second theorem in the same conjunction identifying the Lean heat flow at t = 0 with (1/8) times the classical
  Lambda(s, chi). The proof depends only on propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound and was replayed by five
  checkers from three implementation lineages. Reusable formal components: de Bruijn's theorem for a generic even kernel, Gauss's sign
  theorem (the root number of every real primitive character is 1, in Mathlib's normalisation), and the Mellin
  bridge from the theta kernel to Mathlib's completedLFunction. The conditional statement Lambda_L <= 9/32 under
  a zero-free strip of half-width 3/4 is due to Freire, Gonzalez-Riquelme, Munoz, Poblete and Salinas
  (arXiv:2610.27343); the engine is de Bruijn's 1950 theorem. This is not a new analytic bound and not progress
  on the generalized Riemann hypothesis, which corresponds to Lambda_chi <= 0.
